      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list,\n\\[\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\\]\nwe need to determine the range of values these floor functions take.\n\nEach term in the list is given by \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). The smallest value occurs when \\(n^2\\) is just greater than 1000:\n\\[\nn^2 > 1000 \\implies n > \\sqrt{1000} \\implies n > 31.62.\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\) must be an integer, the smallest \\(n\\) can be is 32. Similarly, the largest value occurs when \\(n^2\\) is just less than 1000000:\n\\[\nn^2 < 1000000 \\implies n < \\sqrt{1000000} \\implies n < 1000.\n\\]\nSince \\(n\\) must be an integer, the largest \\(n\\) can be is 999. Therefore, \\(n\\) ranges from 32 to 999. The number of distinct values of \\(n\\) is:\n\\[\n999 - 32 + 1 = 968.\n\\]\nThus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{968}\\).",
