      "text": "To find the number of distinct numbers in the list \\[\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{2^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{3^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor, \\ \\dots, \\ \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1000^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor,\\] we first simplify each term of the sequence. Each term is given by \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\). We need to determine the range of values \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) can take as \\(n\\) varies from 1 to 1000.\n\nThe smallest value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) occurs when \\(n^2\\) is as small as possible, which is when \\(n = 1\\). So,\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{1^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0.\n\\]\nThe largest value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) occurs when \\(n^2\\) is as large as possible. The largest integer \\(n\\) such that \\(n^2 \\leq 1000\\) is \\(31\\), because \\(31^2 = 961\\) is just under 1000, and \\(32^2 = 1024\\) is just above 1000. So,\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{31^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{961}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0.\n\\]\nFor \\(n = 32\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{32^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1024}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1,\n\\]\nand for \\(n = 33\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{33^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{1089}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1.\n\\]\nIn general, for \\(1 \\leq n \\leq 31\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0,\n\\]\nand for \\(32 \\leq n \\leq 31\\),\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 0,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 1, \\ldots,\n\\]\n\\[\n\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor = 100.\n\\]\nSo, for \\(32 \\leq n \\leq 1000\\), the value of \\(\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n^2}{1000} \\right\\rfloor\\) takes on the values 0 through 99. Therefore, the distinct values are the numbers from 0 to 99, inclusive.\n\nThere are 100 such numbers. Thus, the number of distinct numbers in the list is \\(\\boxed{100}\\).",
